# "What affects the cost" section for each service page, rendered after the main body, and the
# "Questions to ask before you hire" checklist used on the service + city pages.
# No prices are stated here: real pricing depends on the client's rates and each site visit.
# (The instant fence estimate uses the client's own prices from site.config.json.)

COSTS = {
"wood-fences": """<p>Wood fence prices are mostly driven by length, height and the lumber itself: cedar costs more than pressure-treated, and board-on-board or shadowbox styles use more boards per foot than a standard privacy fence. Gates add cost, double driveway gates most of all. Site conditions matter too: slopes that need stepped sections, tree roots, rocky or wet ground, and tight access to the backyard. Removal of an old fence, decorative caps and trim, and staining are priced separately.</p>""",
"vinyl-fences": """<p>Vinyl fence prices depend on the style and the grade of the vinyl: thicker profiles and reinforced rails cost more, as do woodgrain and colour finishes compared with white. Privacy and lattice-top panels cost more per foot than picket styles. Gates with metal reinforcing, extra corners, stepped sections on slopes and short custom-cut panels all add to the price, along with removal of an old fence.</p>""",
"ornamental-fences": """<p>Ornamental fence pricing depends on the material (aluminum or steel), the grade (residential or commercial), height and the style: puppy pickets, rings, scrolls and spear tops add to the base panel price. Racking panels to follow a slope, the number of corners and the number and width of gates all affect the total. Pool-compliant panels and self-closing gate hardware are also factors.</p>""",
"chain-link-fences": """<p>Chain link costs depend on height, the gauge of the fabric and posts, and whether the fabric is galvanized or vinyl-coated. Terminal posts at ends, corners and gates cost more than line posts, so short runs with many corners cost more per foot. Gates, privacy slats, bottom tension wire, security toppings and removal of an old fence are priced separately.</p>""",
"composite-fences": """<p>Composite fence prices depend on the board brand and grade, the post system (aluminum or steel), the height and the design: horizontal boards with metal accents or mixed materials take more time. Trim, caps and gates add to the total, and gates cost more than wood because of the extra weight. Slopes that need stepped sections and removal of an old fence also affect the price.</p>""",
"privacy-fences": """<p>Privacy fence prices depend on the material, the height, the length and how solid the design is: board-on-board and shadowbox styles use more material than a single layer. Wind exposure can mean tighter post spacing or heavier posts. Slopes, gates, decorative tops like lattice, and removal of an old fence are priced separately. Splitting a shared boundary fence with a neighbour is common, and we can provide a written price for both owners.</p>""",
"pool-fences": """<p>Pool fence prices depend on the length of the enclosure, the material and the number of gates, since self-closing hinges and pool-rated latches add cost to each one. Ornamental aluminum is usually priced per panel, vinyl and wood per foot. The permit fee and any deposit are set by the municipality. Tight access around the pool, slopes and coordinating the schedule with a pool installation can also affect the price.</p>""",
"farm-fences": """<p>Rural fence costs depend on the fence type, the total length and the number of corners, ends and gates, because braced assemblies take more material and labour than line posts. Post type (wood or steel) and spacing, wire grade, and rails for horse fencing all change the price per foot. Clearing brush and removing old wire add to the job, as does wet or uneven ground.</p>""",
"commercial-fences": """<p>Commercial fence pricing depends on height, the grade of materials, the total length and the number and type of gates: rolling cantilever gates and gates prepared for operators cost the most. Security toppings, screening, concrete or asphalt cutting, staging around business hours and site access requirements all affect the final price, which is laid out in a written proposal.</p>""",
"custom-gates": """<p>Gate pricing depends on the width, material and style, and on whether new posts are needed. Wide double gates and rolling gates cost much more than walk gates because of the frame, hardware and larger footings. Self-closing hinges, lockable latches, drop rods and anti-sag kits add to the price, as does matching an existing fence.</p>""",
"fence-repair": """<p>Fence repair prices depend on what's broken and how much has to come apart to fix it. Replacing pickets or a panel is a modest job. Replacing posts costs more, especially when old concrete has to be broken out. Gate rebuilds, storm damage to several sections and repairs on fences with hard-to-match materials take more time and parts. We price repairs in writing before starting.</p>""",
"fence-staining": """<p>Staining prices depend on the fence's length and height, whether one side or both sides are stained, and the condition of the wood: weathered or previously stained fences need more cleaning and prep. Solid stains and premium products cost more than basic semi-transparent stains. Repairs found during prep, and access around gardens and structures, also affect the price.</p>""",
"fence-removal": """<p>Fence removal prices depend on the length, the material and the footings: posts set in large concrete footings take much longer to remove than posts set in gravel or soil. Access for hauling, the volume of debris, disposal fees and whether removal is part of a new fence installation all affect the total.</p>""",
}

